              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "Opioids are a broad class of drugs used primarily for pain relief. Where they are derived from depends on whether they are **natural**, **semi-synthetic**, or **fully synthetic**. \n\nThe primary natural source of these compounds is the **opium poppy** (*Papaver somniferum*). \n\n---\n\n### 1. Natural Opioids (Opiates)\nNatural opioids\u2014traditionally referred to as **opiates**\u2014are derived directly from the milky latex (sap) found in the seed pods of the opium poppy plant. The sap is harvested, dried, and refined. \n* **Key examples:** Morphine, codeine, and thebaine.\n\n### 2. Semi-Synthetic Opioids\nSemi-synthetic opioids do not exist directly in nature, but they are derived by **chemically modifying natural opiates** in a laboratory. Scientists take natural poppy-derived compounds and alter their chemical structures to change their potency, duration of action, or side effects.\n* **Heroin** is derived by chemically modifying morphine.\n* **Oxycodone** (OxyContin) and **hydrocodone** (Vicodin) are typically synthesized from thebaine or codeine.\n* **Hydromorphone** (Dilaudid) is synthesized from morphine.\n\n### 3. Fully Synthetic Opioids\nSynthetic opioids are **entirely man-made in chemical laboratories**. They do not use any actual plant matter from the opium poppy. Instead, chemists synthesize them from scratch using raw chemical compounds. However, they are designed to mirror the exact molecular shape of natural opiates so that they can bind to the same opioid receptors in the human brain and spinal cord.\n* **Key examples:** Fentanyl, methadone, and tramadol. \n\n---\n\n### A Note on Terminology: \"Opiates\" vs. \"Opioids\"\n* **Opiates:** Strictly refers only to the naturally occurring, plant-derived compounds (morphine, codeine, opium).\n* **Opioids:** An umbrella term that includes *all* natural, semi-synthetic, and synthetic drugs that bind to the brain's opioid receptors.",
